INT. FIELD HOSPITAL TENT - FLOODED VILLAGE - DAY

Rain drums on canvas. Cots in rows, mud at the edges.

DR. ADAEZE NWOSU (40s, scrubs under a rain poncho) kneels by a cot where a small boy sleeps. She checks his pulse, counts silently.

NURSE BARTEK (30s, big, gentle) holds a drip bag high. He shifts it when she nods.

The boy's mother, LAKSHMI (30s, soaked sari), grips the cot rail. She hasn't blinked in minutes.

OLD MR. FENN (70s, ham-radio volunteer) hovers at the flap with a clipboard.

ADAEZE
His pulse is steady, Lakshmi.

LAKSHMI
He's so cold.

ADAEZE
He will be for a while. That's the water.

She pulls a foil blanket over the boy and tucks it under his chin.

BARTEK
Colour's coming back, Doc.

LAKSHMI
Ravi. Ravi, beta.

She lowers her forehead to the rail and breathes out. Her shoulders drop.

MR. FENN
Doctor Nwosu, the boat's here for the next group.

ADAEZE
Tell them ten minutes, Mr. Fenn.

He nods and leaves. Bartek lowers the drip bag onto a hook.

BARTEK
You want me on the next cot, Doc?

ADAEZE
Tea first, Bartek. You've been standing for six hours.

He grins, tired. She stands, knees cracking, and lays a hand on Lakshmi's back.

FADE OUT.
